1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:02,796
Speaker: You are watching a man
who has spent his entire adult

2
00:00:02,805 --> 00:00:05,396
life losing finally winning.

3
00:00:06,376 --> 00:00:07,526
It is 1988.

4
00:00:08,016 --> 00:00:11,976
His name is Cliff Barnes, and the
company he just took away from his

5
00:00:11,976 --> 00:00:16,366
lifelong rival was stolen from his
own father almost sixty years earlier.

6
00:00:17,296 --> 00:00:19,965
The question is not whether
soap operas do revenge well.

7
00:00:20,695 --> 00:00:22,125
Every soap does revenge.

8
00:00:22,995 --> 00:00:26,585
The better question is why three of the
biggest soaps on American television

9
00:00:27,056 --> 00:00:32,796
in the 1980s kept reaching for one
specific shape of it over and over across

10
00:00:32,796 --> 00:00:34,625
different networks and different decades.

11
00:00:35,755 --> 00:00:39,136
Not a wife getting even with a
husband, not one executive getting

12
00:00:39,145 --> 00:00:43,606
even with another, but a father,
quiet and underestimated for years,

13
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:47,525
finally delivering the payback
nobody in the room saw coming.

14
00:00:48,255 --> 00:00:53,126
The first one starts in Texas in
1930, decades before Dallas ever

15
00:00:53,126 --> 00:00:56,095
aired a single episode of the
story it was actually telling.

16
00:00:56,915 --> 00:01:02,225
A young wildcatter named Digger Barnes met
another wildcatter named Jock Ewing riding

17
00:01:02,226 --> 00:01:04,195
in a boxcar on the way to the oil fields.

18
00:01:04,985 --> 00:01:06,136
They became partners.

19
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:11,135
Digger could smell oil underground
before anyone drilled for it, and

20
00:01:11,135 --> 00:01:14,155
Jock had the business instincts
to turn that talent into money.

21
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:18,756
Together, they built the beginning
of what would become Ewing Oil.

22
00:01:19,745 --> 00:01:24,155
Then Jock put one of their most valuable
fields in his own name alone, claiming

23
00:01:24,156 --> 00:01:27,175
he was protecting the partnership
from Digger's drinking and gambling.

24
00:01:28,215 --> 00:01:30,346
Digger never accepted that explanation.

25
00:01:30,836 --> 00:01:33,455
He spent the rest of his life
insisting his own partner had

26
00:01:33,455 --> 00:01:37,665
swindled him out of half of everything
they had built together, and he

27
00:01:37,665 --> 00:01:39,535
died in 1980 still believing it.

28
00:01:41,006 --> 00:01:43,086
The betrayal was not only financial.

29
00:01:43,566 --> 00:01:47,066
Digger had once been involved with
a woman named Ellie, and by the

30
00:01:47,066 --> 00:01:50,246
time the partnership fell apart,
Jock had married her instead.

31
00:01:51,056 --> 00:01:54,356
So the grudge Digger carried for the
rest of his life was never simply

32
00:01:54,356 --> 00:01:56,016
about a signature on an oil lease.

33
00:01:56,826 --> 00:02:00,336
It was about a friend who had, in
Digger's mind, taken the field, the

34
00:02:00,336 --> 00:02:04,435
fortune, and the woman, and then
had the nerve to build a respected

35
00:02:04,436 --> 00:02:06,155
family name on top of all three.

36
00:02:07,516 --> 00:02:11,325
Decades later, a document actually
surfaced suggesting the three

37
00:02:11,326 --> 00:02:14,906
original partners had split the
company equally after all, and that

38
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:18,485
Jock had only bought the others out
later on ordinary business terms.

39
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:20,896
It did not matter.

40
00:02:21,346 --> 00:02:25,885
By then, Digger was long dead, and the
story the family told itself generation

41
00:02:25,885 --> 00:02:30,075
after generation was the one about the
swindle, not the one about the paperwork.

42
00:02:31,125 --> 00:02:33,995
His son Cliff inherited the
grudge along with the name.

43
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:38,196
For most of the show's run, that
inheritance looked like a joke.

44
00:02:39,585 --> 00:02:42,055
Cliff went up against Jock's son, J.R.

45
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:47,065
Ewing, again and again and lost
again and again, outmaneuvered

46
00:02:47,076 --> 00:02:49,986
by a man who treated business the
way other people played poker.

47
00:02:51,065 --> 00:02:53,556
Audiences watched Cliff
get humiliated for years.

48
00:02:54,366 --> 00:02:57,725
Then in the final season with an
ally named Carter McKay in his

49
00:02:57,726 --> 00:03:01,245
corner, Cliff finally did what
his father never lived to see.

50
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:04,436
He took Ewing Oil away
from the Ewings completely.

51
00:03:05,775 --> 00:03:06,335
J.R.

52
00:03:06,355 --> 00:03:10,095
ended the series alone in his family
home, having lost the company, the

53
00:03:10,095 --> 00:03:14,335
ranch, and his own wife and children,
holding a gun and wondering out loud

54
00:03:14,335 --> 00:03:15,965
if he had ever mattered to anyone.

55
00:03:17,256 --> 00:03:20,805
The revenge that finished the story was
not delivered by the man who was wronged.

56
00:03:21,985 --> 00:03:26,425
It was delivered by his son almost
six decades later, which is the first

57
00:03:26,425 --> 00:03:27,995
thing these stories have in common.

58
00:03:28,905 --> 00:03:32,345
The payback does not need to arrive
on the same lifetime as the grievance.

59
00:03:33,215 --> 00:03:36,646
It only needs someone left standing
who is willing to collect it, no

60
00:03:36,646 --> 00:03:40,135
matter how long that takes or how many
times that person loses along the way.

61
00:03:41,065 --> 00:03:44,595
The second story starts quieter,
and that is exactly the point.

62
00:03:45,546 --> 00:03:49,095
In nineteen eighty-two, a new character
arrived in the fictional town of Salem,

63
00:03:49,455 --> 00:03:53,716
calling himself simply the father of a
man already living there, a businessman

64
00:03:53,726 --> 00:03:55,395
looking to expand into a new city.

65
00:03:56,735 --> 00:03:59,145
Nothing about the introduction
suggested a threat.

66
00:03:59,645 --> 00:04:03,486
His name was Stefano DiMera, and the
man he had actually come to destroy

67
00:04:03,486 --> 00:04:07,695
was a police officer named Roman
Brady, who had once worked with an

68
00:04:07,705 --> 00:04:11,695
international agency on an operation
aimed at bringing Stefano down.

69
00:04:13,112 --> 00:04:17,512
Stefano knew exactly who Roman was,
and Roman had every reason to assume

70
00:04:17,512 --> 00:04:19,022
the danger had already passed.

71
00:04:20,372 --> 00:04:23,272
A businessman moving to town
to be near his son did not

72
00:04:23,272 --> 00:04:24,962
read as a declaration of war.

73
00:04:25,712 --> 00:04:28,232
That misjudgment was the entire strategy.

74
00:04:29,631 --> 00:04:31,692
Stefano did not need Roman to fear him.

75
00:04:32,071 --> 00:04:34,662
He needed Roman to stop
watching him, and a friendly

76
00:04:34,662 --> 00:04:36,461
introduction bought exactly that.

77
00:04:37,311 --> 00:04:41,511
He watched and he waited, and in nineteen
eighty-three, he made his first move.

78
00:04:42,231 --> 00:04:46,981
He had an associate who he had surgically
altered to resemble his own son so he

79
00:04:46,982 --> 00:04:51,082
could move through the family undetected,
help frame Roman for a string of

80
00:04:51,151 --> 00:04:55,692
unsolved killings the town had come
to call the Salem Strangler murders.

81
00:04:57,134 --> 00:05:01,404
The most efficient revenge in this story
never announced itself as revenge at all.

82
00:05:02,174 --> 00:05:04,874
It borrowed a family member's face
to get through the front door.

83
00:05:05,414 --> 00:05:06,973
That was only the opening move.

84
00:05:07,804 --> 00:05:11,644
In nineteen eighty-four, Stefano
lured Roman into a confrontation on

85
00:05:11,644 --> 00:05:16,044
a cliffside, and Roman fell, presumed
dead in his own brother's arms.

86
00:05:16,724 --> 00:05:21,234
What the Brady family did not know was
that Stefano had not simply let him die.

87
00:05:21,933 --> 00:05:26,144
He had taken the body and in its place
inserted a man who had been secretly

88
00:05:26,144 --> 00:05:31,053
brainwashed into believing he actually was
Roman, sending that stranger back into the

89
00:05:31,053 --> 00:05:33,253
family to live a life that was not his.

90
00:05:33,783 --> 00:05:38,773
For years, a family grieved a man who
might not have been entirely gone while

91
00:05:38,773 --> 00:05:42,544
unknowingly welcoming someone else's
mind wearing his face into their home.

92
00:05:43,913 --> 00:05:46,694
Then in nineteen eighty-five,
Stefano escalated again.

93
00:05:47,443 --> 00:05:50,934
Blaming the Bradys for the death of his
own daughter, he kidnapped Roman's infant

94
00:05:50,934 --> 00:05:55,873
twins, a boy and a girl barely old enough
to be christened as the next installment

95
00:05:55,873 --> 00:05:59,374
in a revenge campaign that showed no
sign of considering itself finished.

96
00:06:00,044 --> 00:06:04,404
It would take until nineteen ninety-one
for Stefano to even admit out loud what he

97
00:06:04,404 --> 00:06:09,083
had actually done, that the disappearance,
the double, and the brainwashing had

98
00:06:09,134 --> 00:06:10,904
all been his design from the start.

99
00:06:12,244 --> 00:06:15,163
By the time that confession aired,
the audience had already spent

100
00:06:15,174 --> 00:06:17,134
years assuming the worst was over.

101
00:06:18,053 --> 00:06:20,423
That is the turn in this entire story.

102
00:06:21,253 --> 00:06:25,223
This was never one father delivering
one act of payback in one episode.

103
00:06:25,884 --> 00:06:29,953
It was a revenge campaign that spanned
years, spanned identities, and eventually

104
00:06:29,954 --> 00:06:33,393
reached children who had not even been
born when the original grudge began.

105
00:06:34,163 --> 00:06:36,744
Digger Barnes wanted one oil field back.

106
00:06:36,744 --> 00:06:42,473
Stefano DiMera wanted a family destroyed
across generations, and he was patient

107
00:06:42,473 --> 00:06:46,393
enough to build the destruction one
scheme at a time rather than rush it.

108
00:06:47,803 --> 00:06:51,614
None of this happened by accident, and
it did not happen because soap writers

109
00:06:51,614 --> 00:06:53,164
were simply obsessed with fathers.

110
00:06:53,893 --> 00:06:57,963
It happened because of who was watching
and because of how much time these shows

111
00:06:58,013 --> 00:06:59,983
actually had to spend a grudge properly.

112
00:07:00,744 --> 00:07:04,903
The audience for daytime drama in this
era was overwhelmingly women managing

113
00:07:04,903 --> 00:07:08,803
households where they were routinely
underestimated by the men making decisions

114
00:07:08,803 --> 00:07:13,354
around them in business, in money,
and whose judgment counted in a room.

115
00:07:14,446 --> 00:07:17,496
A father figure who gets quietly
dismissed for years and then

116
00:07:17,496 --> 00:07:19,286
wins anyway is not just a plot.

117
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:23,386
It is a stand-in for every viewer
who has ever been counted out and

118
00:07:23,386 --> 00:07:27,226
wanted, just once, to watch someone
like them prove everybody wrong.

119
00:07:28,036 --> 00:07:30,706
And these stories had the
runway to make that payoff feel

120
00:07:30,805 --> 00:07:32,185
earned instead of convenient.

121
00:07:33,136 --> 00:07:36,176
A weekly primetime drama gets
roughly twenty-two episodes a

122
00:07:36,176 --> 00:07:37,646
year to build toward a reversal.

123
00:07:38,585 --> 00:07:42,665
A daily soap produced that many episodes
in about a month, which meant a grudge

124
00:07:42,665 --> 00:07:47,306
like Digger's or a scheme like Stefano's
could breathe across real calendar

125
00:07:47,306 --> 00:07:51,715
years with actors visibly aging and
children growing up on screen the

126
00:07:51,716 --> 00:07:53,976
same way they do in an actual family.

127
00:07:54,505 --> 00:07:57,395
By the time the payoff finally
landed, it did not feel like a twist

128
00:07:57,396 --> 00:07:59,036
a writer invented on a deadline.

129
00:07:59,665 --> 00:08:02,246
It felt like something that had
been earning its own weight the

130
00:08:02,246 --> 00:08:03,865
entire time you were watching.

131
00:08:04,446 --> 00:08:07,966
There is also a simpler, more
practical reason these particular

132
00:08:07,966 --> 00:08:09,296
storylines kept recurring.

133
00:08:10,735 --> 00:08:14,856
A father with money, a business,
or a family name was the character

134
00:08:14,856 --> 00:08:18,045
who had the most to lose and
the most tools to lose it with.

135
00:08:19,495 --> 00:08:21,275
A wronged son could get angry.

136
00:08:21,846 --> 00:08:25,665
A wronged father could quietly
rewrite a will, redirect a company,

137
00:08:25,956 --> 00:08:29,106
or spend a decade building a
case nobody else knew existed.

138
00:08:29,575 --> 00:08:33,996
Revenge needs leverage, and on
these shows, leverage almost always

139
00:08:34,026 --> 00:08:36,926
belonged to the men who already
controlled the oil field, the

140
00:08:36,926 --> 00:08:38,945
corporation, or the family fortune.

141
00:08:40,205 --> 00:08:43,285
Underestimating one of them was
not just a personal misjudgment.

142
00:08:43,766 --> 00:08:47,595
It was a strategic one, and the
whole plot existed to punish it.

143
00:08:48,835 --> 00:08:53,216
That is also why almost nobody who did not
grow up with these shows would guess how

144
00:08:53,216 --> 00:08:55,375
enormous these storylines actually were.

145
00:08:55,905 --> 00:08:58,695
A twist that unfolds over
one episode is a plot point.

146
00:08:59,175 --> 00:09:03,195
A twist that unfolds over five
years told across a format most of

147
00:09:03,196 --> 00:09:06,566
the television industry treated as
disposable does not get remembered

148
00:09:06,566 --> 00:09:08,215
as a great piece of storytelling.

149
00:09:09,115 --> 00:09:11,965
It gets remembered, if at all,
as something your mother used

150
00:09:11,965 --> 00:09:13,075
to watch in the afternoon.

151
00:09:14,516 --> 00:09:17,106
Cliff Barnes waited almost sixty years.

152
00:09:17,595 --> 00:09:19,985
Stefano DiMera never really
stopped waiting at all.

153
00:09:20,475 --> 00:09:23,995
On these shows, patience was
never treated as a virtue standing

154
00:09:23,996 --> 00:09:25,626
quietly off to the side of the plot.

155
00:09:26,375 --> 00:09:30,235
It was the plot running underneath
every other storyline for as long

156
00:09:30,235 --> 00:09:31,656
as the network kept the lights on.
